1
00:00:00,190 --> 00:00:01,940
Greetings, fellow aliens!

2
00:00:01,940 --> 00:00:08,000
Today, I will explain something that puzzles
many alien visitors: Funny situations, jokes,

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:43,289
and laughing - in one word: Humour.

4
00:00:43,289 --> 00:00:47,609
My videos are usually based on a mix of earthling
literature, alien sources and conclusions

5
00:00:47,609 --> 00:00:49,249
drawn from my own observations.

6
00:00:49,249 --> 00:00:54,350
Humour, however, is a tricky thing which puzzles
alien and earthling scientists alike.

7
00:00:54,350 --> 00:01:00,059
So, this episode is entirely based on an earthling
book released one solar cycle ago: "Inside

8
00:01:00,059 --> 00:01:02,859
Jokes - Using Humor to Reverse-Engineer the
Mind".

9
00:01:02,859 --> 00:01:06,550
It provides the most convincing theory about
humour I've seen so far.

10
00:01:06,550 --> 00:01:09,230
You'll find a link in the description below.

11
00:01:09,230 --> 00:01:13,650
Let's start with a typical funny situation:
An earthling crosses a river, a body of hydric

12
00:01:13,650 --> 00:01:18,490
acid, by walking on stones, when one of the
stones turns out to be an animal.

13
00:01:18,490 --> 00:01:23,410
This mistake triggers a peculiar reaction:
The earthling produces strange rhythmic noises,

14
00:01:23,410 --> 00:01:24,410
called laughing.

15
00:01:24,410 --> 00:01:26,940
The whole phenomenon is called humour.

16
00:01:26,940 --> 00:01:31,040
Early alien visitors of earth thought that
laughing is an artificial respiratory distress

17
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:34,280
induced by the brain, in order to punish mistakes.

18
00:01:34,280 --> 00:01:39,600
However, this can't be right, for earthlings
actually enjoy laughing - which is very strange.

19
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:44,530
Anyway, humour seems to be triggered by mistakes,
based on false assumptions.

20
00:01:44,530 --> 00:01:48,630
To understand what is going on, we have first
to understand the important role of assumptions

21
00:01:48,630 --> 00:01:50,900
for the earthling mind.

22
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:54,140
Earthlings are often compared to levy drones
of the tax authority.

23
00:01:54,140 --> 00:01:58,030
Like drones, earthlings create a model of
the environment in their mind, in order to

24
00:01:58,030 --> 00:01:59,530
make decisions.

25
00:01:59,530 --> 00:02:01,550
This model is called "mental space".

26
00:02:01,550 --> 00:02:06,780
Unlike drones, an Earthling is even capable
of handling several mental spaces simultaneously.

27
00:02:06,780 --> 00:02:11,870
For example, one mental space for the present,
one for last night, one for the near future,

28
00:02:11,870 --> 00:02:14,680
and one for a video game he is currently playing.

29
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:20,010
However, drones perform continuously full-spectrum
scans of the environment, whereas the earthlings'

30
00:02:20,010 --> 00:02:23,250
rudimentary senses provide very incomplete
data.

31
00:02:23,250 --> 00:02:27,350
In consequence, the earthlings have to fill
the gaps by making educated guesses.

32
00:02:27,350 --> 00:02:30,180
In other words: Assumptions.

33
00:02:30,180 --> 00:02:31,990
A second problem is time.

34
00:02:31,990 --> 00:02:35,980
No matter whether in an airplane or in a bar,
Earthlings have often no choice but to make

35
00:02:35,980 --> 00:02:39,850
decisions in a limited amount of time, based
on incomplete data.

36
00:02:39,850 --> 00:02:46,190
So, assumptions are unavoidable, but unfortunately,
false assumptions can have fatal consequences.

37
00:02:46,190 --> 00:02:47,420
That's where humour comes in.

38
00:02:47,420 --> 00:02:50,490
Let's try to define what humour actually is.

39
00:02:50,490 --> 00:02:54,510
Humour seems to be triggered by the discovery
of a false assumption - like the assumption

40
00:02:54,510 --> 00:02:56,709
that the thing in the river is a stone.

41
00:02:56,709 --> 00:02:58,970
But not every false assumption is funny.

42
00:02:58,970 --> 00:03:03,020
For example, if the earthling is just sitting
at the shore of the river, sees what might

43
00:03:03,020 --> 00:03:07,510
or might not be a stone, but doesn't decide
what to believe, it's not funny.

44
00:03:07,510 --> 00:03:11,240
He has to commit to the false assumption;
he has to take it as truth rather than a mere

45
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:12,240
possibility.

46
00:03:12,240 --> 00:03:16,530
Secondly, if he commits to a false assumption
which leads to fatal consequences like losing

47
00:03:16,530 --> 00:03:20,860
his legs, it's not funny either - at least
not for him, right now.

48
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:25,110
More generally, it's not funny if the discovery
of the mistake is loaded with negative emotions.

49
00:03:25,110 --> 00:03:29,410
So, humour only occurs when the earthling
commits to an assumption which turns out to

50
00:03:29,410 --> 00:03:31,310
be false, but harmless.

51
00:03:31,310 --> 00:03:35,349
Thirdly, if the earthling wonders consciously
whether the thing in the river is an animal,

52
00:03:35,349 --> 00:03:39,510
then decides it's a stone, it's not funny
either to discover it's an animal.

53
00:03:39,510 --> 00:03:42,850
It's necessary that the assumption has entered
the mental space covertly.

54
00:03:42,850 --> 00:03:46,520
Let me explain this in a typically earthling
manner: With a metaphor.

55
00:03:46,520 --> 00:03:49,420
Let's say, this is the earthling mind.

56
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:53,319
Many assumptions enter the mental space by
the font-door, are examined and analyzed,

57
00:03:53,319 --> 00:03:55,680
before the mind accepts or refuses them.

58
00:03:55,680 --> 00:03:59,040
But some assumptions enter the mind by the
backdoor and establish themselves without

59
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:00,880
having been thought over.

60
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:05,180
Such sneaky assumptions are vicious, if the
mind trusts them, as they can do a lot of

61
00:04:05,180 --> 00:04:06,180
harm.

62
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:08,420
Now we can define what humour is.

63
00:04:08,420 --> 00:04:12,830
It's the discovery of those sneaky false backdoor
assumptions before they can do any harm.

64
00:04:12,830 --> 00:04:14,800
So, humour occurs when :

65
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:17,799
A) An assumption enters the mental space covertly.

66
00:04:17,799 --> 00:04:18,799
B)

67
00:04:18,799 --> 00:04:20,090
The mind takes it to be true.

68
00:04:20,090 --> 00:04:22,550
C) The assumption is revealed as false.

69
00:04:22,550 --> 00:04:27,300
D) The mistake is discovered without causing
harm or strong negative emotions.

70
00:04:27,300 --> 00:04:32,050
To be exact, the earthling book this episode
is based upon doesn't speak of assumptions,

71
00:04:32,050 --> 00:04:35,160
but of active beliefs, which is a bit more
precise.

72
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:40,180
But as often, I had to simplify some things
in order to keep the video accessible to anybody.

73
00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:43,770
There are two other species in the galaxy
which have a similar sense of humour.

74
00:04:43,770 --> 00:04:46,050
The difference to earthlings is that they
hate it.

75
00:04:46,050 --> 00:04:51,139
Both species live in the Aronian system and
have been supervised by the same bio administrator.

76
00:04:51,139 --> 00:04:54,880
Apparently, this administrator thought it
would be a good idea to have their mind punish

77
00:04:54,880 --> 00:04:57,169
them for discovering false assumptions.

78
00:04:57,169 --> 00:05:00,770
The result, however, was a disaster.

79
00:05:00,770 --> 00:05:04,630
One of the species, the Cunctators, became
obsessive with double- and triple checking

80
00:05:04,630 --> 00:05:09,390
everything before making the smallest decision
- even when they were, for example, chased

81
00:05:09,390 --> 00:05:11,370
by a hoard of furious enemies.

82
00:05:11,370 --> 00:05:15,300
This way they avoided some mistakes, but they
became incredibly slow and unable to make

83
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:18,090
decisions under pressure.

84
00:05:18,090 --> 00:05:19,522
Their world eventually became a planet of
bureaucrats.

85
00:05:19,522 --> 00:05:26,600
The second species, the Schildarians, became
masters of not recognizing errors.

86
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:30,870
The guildhall of the planetary capital, for
example, has no windows because the builders

87
00:05:30,870 --> 00:05:32,260
forgot to build them.

88
00:05:32,260 --> 00:05:34,949
But everybody pretends that everything is
fine.

89
00:05:34,949 --> 00:05:38,949
The cities of the planet are full of such
architectural errors, which makes it a popular

90
00:05:38,949 --> 00:05:40,930
destination for tourists.

91
00:05:40,930 --> 00:05:45,360
The earthling humour works much better: Instead
of punishing false assumptions, it rewards

92
00:05:45,360 --> 00:05:49,290
defusing those sneaky false assumptions before
they can do any harm.

93
00:05:49,290 --> 00:05:53,229
The goal is probably to avoid such mistakes
in the future without discouraging the earthling

94
00:05:53,229 --> 00:05:55,040
from making assumptions.

95
00:05:55,040 --> 00:05:57,100
But what about other peoples' mistakes?

96
00:05:57,100 --> 00:06:00,790
Well, watching someone else committing to
a sneaky false assumption is equally funny

97
00:06:00,790 --> 00:06:05,400
- even more if the observer discovers the
mistake before the actor falls for it.

98
00:06:05,400 --> 00:06:09,520
It's even funny if the mistake is harmful,
as long as the observer doesn't care.

99
00:06:09,520 --> 00:06:14,100
However, death is rarely funny, except for
fictional characters or people the observer

100
00:06:14,100 --> 00:06:15,919
doesn't care about at all.

101
00:06:15,919 --> 00:06:20,070
That's because earthlings consider death as
something tragic, even when someone dies of

102
00:06:20,070 --> 00:06:21,070
old age.

103
00:06:21,070 --> 00:06:25,979
This is strange, as earthlings and virtually
all creatures on earth are biologically programmed

104
00:06:25,979 --> 00:06:28,070
to die some day.

105
00:06:28,070 --> 00:06:32,759
Except for the microbes.

106
00:06:32,759 --> 00:06:37,759
Now we understand why earthlings enjoy humour:
It's a reward for defusing false sneaky backdoor

107
00:06:37,759 --> 00:06:40,630
assumptions, before they can do any harm.

108
00:06:40,630 --> 00:06:45,040
But that doesn't explain the strange noises
earthlings make when encountering humour.

109
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:46,889
Those noises are called laughing.

110
00:06:46,889 --> 00:06:51,169
To understand this, we have to examine two
other things which may trigger laughing: Playing

111
00:06:51,169 --> 00:06:52,320
and tickling.

112
00:06:52,320 --> 00:06:57,130
In episode 5 we have learned that earthlings,
especially earthling cubs, are used to simulate

113
00:06:57,130 --> 00:06:59,990
danger situations for training purposes.

114
00:06:59,990 --> 00:07:01,280
This is called playing.

115
00:07:01,280 --> 00:07:05,889
Now, bystanders may mistake playing for real
danger, especially if the players emit alert

116
00:07:05,889 --> 00:07:08,880
signals such as screaming or calling for help.

117
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:12,889
That's why earthling nature developed a "false
alarm" signal: Laughing.

118
00:07:12,889 --> 00:07:16,400
When an earthling hears alert signals mixed
with laughing, he understands that the alert

119
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:20,169
is not serious and that the danger only exists
in the fictional world of the game.

120
00:07:20,169 --> 00:07:26,190
Odds are that this "no danger" signal is the
original purpose of laughing.

121
00:07:26,190 --> 00:07:29,639
To understand tickling, we need some background
information.

122
00:07:29,639 --> 00:07:32,539
It has to do with two feelings: Pain and itching.

123
00:07:32,539 --> 00:07:35,430
Pain is a very unpleasant alert feeling following
an injury.

124
00:07:35,430 --> 00:07:38,889
Severe pain usually triggers screaming and
calling for help.

125
00:07:38,889 --> 00:07:42,810
Itching is another alert feeling indicating
the presence of a parasite on the skin, it

126
00:07:42,810 --> 00:07:45,130
triggers scratching to remove the parasite.

127
00:07:45,130 --> 00:07:50,100
Itching is less unpleasant than pain, except
when lasting for a prolonged period of time.

128
00:07:50,100 --> 00:07:51,860
But what has this to do with laughing?

129
00:07:51,860 --> 00:07:56,350
Well, serious combat between earthlings, as
well as ritualized combat, usually has the

130
00:07:56,350 --> 00:07:58,069
goal to inflict pain.

131
00:07:58,069 --> 00:08:03,160
But playful combat, especially between earthling
cubs, aims at itching rather than pain, by

132
00:08:03,160 --> 00:08:05,630
touching sensible areas with the fingers.

133
00:08:05,630 --> 00:08:09,729
This is called tickling, and the usual reaction
is screaming mixed with laughing, in order

134
00:08:09,729 --> 00:08:11,360
not to alert bystanders.

135
00:08:11,360 --> 00:08:15,980
Again, laughter is used as a signal that there
is no serious problem.

136
00:08:15,980 --> 00:08:20,770
So initially, laughing is a communication
signal, more precisely a signal that indicates

137
00:08:20,770 --> 00:08:21,819
"false alarm".

138
00:08:21,819 --> 00:08:25,690
Now, a surprise like the animal in the river
often triggers screaming before the earthling

139
00:08:25,690 --> 00:08:30,520
realizes that there is no real danger, so
the earthling laughs to cancel the alarm.

140
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:35,820
That's possibly the origin of laughing as
reaction to humour.

141
00:08:35,820 --> 00:08:36,820
Scientific advice.

142
00:08:36,820 --> 00:08:41,409
If you want to examine laughing, it may prove
difficult to obtain it with humour, as your

143
00:08:41,409 --> 00:08:43,789
test subjects will probably be terrified by
the situation.

144
00:08:43,789 --> 00:08:47,010
You might try out other methods like tickling
or cannabis.

145
00:08:47,010 --> 00:08:51,610
But the simplest way of making an earthling
laugh is by making them inhale nitrous oxide,

146
00:08:51,610 --> 00:08:55,700
known on earth as laughing gas.

147
00:08:55,700 --> 00:08:59,570
The alarm signal of screaming is actually
"infectious": Screaming earthlings may make

148
00:08:59,570 --> 00:09:03,320
other earthlings scream, even if they don't
know what is going on.

149
00:09:03,320 --> 00:09:07,160
This is called panic, and it's a mechanism
to alert a group quickly in case of imminent

150
00:09:07,160 --> 00:09:08,160
danger.

151
00:09:08,160 --> 00:09:11,940
Now, if the "false alarm" signal of laughing
has the purpose of cancelling the screaming

152
00:09:11,940 --> 00:09:14,630
alarm, it has to propagate in a similar way.

153
00:09:14,630 --> 00:09:20,490
That's probably why laughter is also infectious.

154
00:09:20,490 --> 00:09:22,200
Strategic advice.

155
00:09:22,200 --> 00:09:25,110
When you invade earth, panic can be a powerful
weapon.

156
00:09:25,110 --> 00:09:29,560
An effective way of creating panic is to terrorize
earthlings with mighty war machines.

157
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:33,520
However, when you hear earthlings laughing
at their approach, your war machines might

158
00:09:33,520 --> 00:09:35,620
not be as terrifying as you thought.

159
00:09:35,620 --> 00:09:43,180
One reason might be that you have underestimated
the size of earthlings.

160
00:09:43,180 --> 00:09:46,850
As mentioned before, funny situations are
rewarded by pleasure.

161
00:09:46,850 --> 00:09:50,440
But earthlings wouldn't be earthlings if they
hadn't found ways of creating this pleasure

162
00:09:50,440 --> 00:09:51,510
artificially.

163
00:09:51,510 --> 00:09:55,120
In its simplest form, the artificial humour
is called jokes.

164
00:09:55,120 --> 00:09:58,800
A joke is typically a funny story, question
or remark.

165
00:09:58,800 --> 00:10:02,090
Most jokes feature a fool falling for a false
assumption.

166
00:10:02,090 --> 00:10:04,721
Sometimes the fool is in the joke.

167
00:10:04,721 --> 00:10:06,730
Occasionally the teller pretends to be the
fool.

168
00:10:06,730 --> 00:10:10,930
But more sophisticated jokes make the listener
the fool, by tricking him into making a false

169
00:10:10,930 --> 00:10:11,930
assumption.

170
00:10:11,930 --> 00:10:14,120
An example for the fool being inside the joke:

171
00:10:14,120 --> 00:10:16,470
"How do you get a Polari in a Q3 Mini Saucer?"

172
00:10:16,470 --> 00:10:20,240
"Tell him 16 of his kind are already inside."

173
00:10:20,240 --> 00:10:22,850
In this case, the Polari is the fool.

174
00:10:22,850 --> 00:10:26,660
Of course, the joke isn't funny if you don't
know about the Polari culture's obsession

175
00:10:26,660 --> 00:10:28,480
with the number 17.

176
00:10:28,480 --> 00:10:33,001
And even if I explain it, it won't get funnier:
Explaining a joke is like dissecting an earthling

177
00:10:33,001 --> 00:10:36,490
- few people enjoy it, and the earthling dies.

178
00:10:36,490 --> 00:10:37,490
Other examples:

179
00:10:37,490 --> 00:10:40,070
"How do you get four squids in a Q3 Mini Saucer?"

180
00:10:40,070 --> 00:10:43,890
"One on the pilot seat, and three on the passenger
seats."

181
00:10:43,890 --> 00:10:47,830
In this case, the fool is the teller who pretends
not seeing that the problem is the size of

182
00:10:47,830 --> 00:10:49,880
the squids, not the number of seats.

183
00:10:49,880 --> 00:10:53,000
"How do you get three Andromeda lawyers in
a Q3 Mini Saucer?"

184
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:56,269
"You can't, because the squids are still inside."

185
00:10:56,269 --> 00:11:00,570
In this case, the listener is the fool, because
he was led into assuming that the two jokes

186
00:11:00,570 --> 00:11:04,670
are not connected, or in other words, that
they don't share the same mental space.

187
00:11:04,670 --> 00:11:07,440
"How do you get t(w)o Polaris in a Q3 Mini
Saucer?"

188
00:11:07,440 --> 00:11:11,550
"Via hyperspace express line 37, exit Polaris."

189
00:11:11,550 --> 00:11:15,200
Again, the listener is the fool who misunderstood
the question.

190
00:11:15,200 --> 00:11:19,589
This is a pun, a joke that plays with the
ambiguity between words - in this case between

191
00:11:19,589 --> 00:11:23,040
the plural of Polari, and the star Polaris.

192
00:11:23,040 --> 00:11:27,490
Now some more examples for my earthling viewers,
who don't know anything about Polari culture

193
00:11:27,490 --> 00:11:29,290
and hyperspace express lines.

194
00:11:29,290 --> 00:11:31,730
"How do you know there is a squid in your
refrigerator?

195
00:11:31,730 --> 00:11:32,730
"

196
00:11:32,730 --> 00:11:34,410
"By the tentacle traces on the butter."

197
00:11:34,410 --> 00:11:37,040
How do you know there are two squids in the
refrigerator?

198
00:11:37,040 --> 00:11:39,150
You can hear them giggle when the light goes
out."

199
00:11:39,150 --> 00:11:42,140
"How do you know there are three squids in
the refrigerator?

200
00:11:42,140 --> 00:11:44,010
You can't quite close the door."

201
00:11:44,010 --> 00:11:46,300
"How do you know there are four squids in
the refrigerator?"

202
00:11:46,300 --> 00:11:50,149
"There is an empty Q3 Mini Saucer parked outside..."

203
00:11:50,149 --> 00:11:52,800
Another form of artificial humour are comedies.

204
00:11:52,800 --> 00:11:56,730
Comedies are full-fledged stories full of
jokes and funny situations.

205
00:11:56,730 --> 00:12:02,550
Like all stories, they are created in the
form of novels, stage plays, films, etc.

206
00:12:02,550 --> 00:12:06,060
There is an ongoing discussion among alien
scientists whether the movie "Prometheus"

207
00:12:06,060 --> 00:12:07,490
is actually a comedy.

208
00:12:07,490 --> 00:12:09,930
On one side it follows the classical comedy
formula:

209
00:12:09,930 --> 00:12:12,920
"Some idiots go some place and do stupid things".

210
00:12:12,920 --> 00:12:16,589
But on the other side, earthlings don't laugh
when watching the movie.

211
00:12:16,589 --> 00:12:21,310
My hypothesis is that Prometheus is actually
a safety video for future astronauts.

212
00:12:21,310 --> 00:12:25,079
The goal is to warn about stupid mistakes
one can make in a space mission:

213
00:12:25,079 --> 00:12:26,709
Don't hire morons!

214
00:12:26,709 --> 00:12:28,959
Don't pet creepy alien creatures!

215
00:12:28,959 --> 00:12:31,360
Don't smuggle alien goo on board!

216
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:33,810
Don't test alien goo on your fellow astronauts!

217
00:12:33,810 --> 00:12:38,580
And, for Galaxy's sake: Don't disturb the
siesta of a giant alien who wants to kill

218
00:12:38,580 --> 00:12:40,830
all earthlings!!

219
00:12:40,830 --> 00:12:42,260
Tips for tourists.

220
00:12:42,260 --> 00:12:45,240
Usually, earthlings will be afraid when they
see you.

221
00:12:45,240 --> 00:12:48,959
But when you visit North America during this
time of the year, odds are that you will make

222
00:12:48,959 --> 00:12:50,500
earthlings laugh.

223
00:12:50,500 --> 00:12:54,150
The reason is an annual ritualized game where
earthling cubs disguise as creatures of the

224
00:12:54,150 --> 00:12:56,770
night and grown-ups pretend to be scared by
them.

225
00:12:56,770 --> 00:13:00,779
The cubs find this funny, despite the fact
that the mistake only occurs in the make-belief

226
00:13:00,779 --> 00:13:02,390
world of the game.

227
00:13:02,390 --> 00:13:05,910
Anyway, earthlings will probably think you
are a disguised earthling cub.

228
00:13:05,910 --> 00:13:10,490
You might even make new friends and earn some
free glucose.

229
00:13:10,490 --> 00:13:13,540
Earthlings usually think of humour as some
kind of gratuitous pleasure.

230
00:13:13,540 --> 00:13:18,030
But from an evolutionary point of view, it
has a very specific purpose: Training to defuse

231
00:13:18,030 --> 00:13:21,950
those nasty false backdoor assumptions, before
they can do any harm.

232
00:13:21,950 --> 00:13:29,920
So, once again, it all comes down to our old
friend, the genetic imperative.

233
00:13:29,920 --> 00:13:32,860
In the next episode we will learn about earthlings
food:

234
00:13:32,860 --> 00:13:37,210
What they eat and drink, how they prepare
it, and why they love spoiled food like cheese

235
00:13:37,210 --> 00:13:38,380
or wine.

236
00:13:38,380 --> 00:13:59,670
Thanks for watching, and don't forget to be
alien!

